1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
This video recording is on disk space management.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:13,000
One important issue in file space allocation is to determine the block size.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:19,000
Block size affects both data read and disk space utilization.

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:24,000
There is a trade-off between data read and space utilization.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,000
If block size is large, a file will occupy less number of blocks.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
To read the file, in this case, will require less number of IOs.

7
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,000
This translates to fast data read.

8
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:50,000
However, it is waste space since the last data block of the file may not occupy the entire disk block.

9
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:59,000
So recall, no matter which file allocation method is used, they all suffer from internal fragmentation.

10
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:02,000
And this happened to the last block.

11
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:11,000
If the block size is small, then a file will occupy many blocks.

12
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Then to read the file, for example, that will require a large number of disk IOs.

13
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:25,000
So the data read is slow, but the wastage on the last block will be small.

14
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:33,000
The trade-off between the data read and space utilization is graphically illustrated on this slide.

15
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,000
So there are two curves.

16
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:41,000
One for the data read and the other one is for utilization.

17
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:49,000
So you can clearly see the opposite trend of the curve when the block size increases.

18
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:57,000
So to deal with this trade-off, some OIs actually use two block sizes for file allocation.

19
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:04,000
And this will be further discussed in a tutorial question.

20
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Just as the space that is allocated to the file must be managed, so the space that is not currently in use, right, not currently allocated to any file must also be managed.

21
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:34,000
To perform any of the file allocation techniques described previously, then it is necessary to know which blocks on the disk are currently available.

22
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:46,000
So another major responsibility of the file organization module is to keep track of the free space on the hard disk.

23
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,000
So in general, there are two different approaches.

24
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
So the bitmap or the linked list.

25
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:03,000
So using a bitmap, each block on the hard disk is represented by one bit.

26
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:09,000
So if a block is free, then the corresponding bit will be zero.

27
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:21,000
However, if the block is occupied, allocated to a file, then the corresponding bit for the block will be one.

28
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:23,000
So this is the same block.

29
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:30,000
However, the bitmap also occupies storage space and can be quite big.

30
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:40,000
So for instance, given the block size of 2.9 bytes, so that's 512 bytes.

31
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:50,000
So given this block size, and given the hardware size is 2.34 bytes, so this is a 16GB byte.

32
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:51,000
So this is a 16GB byte.

33
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:52,000
It's quite small, right.

34
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:57,000
Then the number of blocks in this hard disk will be 2.34 divided by 2.9.

35
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:02,000
So this gives you a number of blocks.

36
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:07,000
In other words, the number of bits required.

37
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:09,000
So 8 bits per byte.

38
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:13,000
So divided by 8, then we have 2.22 bytes.

39
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:22,000
So for this small hard disk, then it will require 2.22 bytes storage space for the bitmap.

40
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:24,000
So this is a 4 megabyte.

41
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:33,000
Bitmap is usually kept in a fixed location on the hard disk.

42
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:39,000
So to improve performance, it can be cached in memory.

43
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:43,000
So the example discussed in the last slide.

44
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:47,000
The size of bitmap is 4 megabytes.

45
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:53,000
Now the question is, can we spare 4 megabyte of memory for this bitmap?

46
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:00,000
So if so, then the bitmap can be searched without need for accessing the hard disk.

47
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:04,000
So we can cache the entire bitmap in the memory.

48
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:16,000
So if we keep a copy of the bitmap in the memory, then in order to keep the copy of this bitmap on hard disk, right, consistent.

49
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:22,000
Then the bitmap in the memory needs to be written back to the hard disk occasionally.

50
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:24,000
So recall the concept of cache, right?

51
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:27,000
So we have 2 copies of bitmap.

52
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:32,000
One on hard disk, the other one is in the memory.

53
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:42,000
So whenever the version in the memory is updated, then we also need to update the version on the hard disk to maintain consistency.

54
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:54,000
So even when the bitmap is in the memory, an exhaustive search of the map can also introduce considerable overhead.

55
00:05:54,000 --> 00:06:00,000
Considering what needs to be done when a file is created or deleted.

56
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:11,000
So for example, so assuming contiguous file allocation, so to create a file, then it requires n blocks.

57
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:19,000
Operating system then needs to search the bitmap to locate n consecutive bits whose value is 1.

58
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:21,000
So n three available blocks.

59
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:31,000
Then to delete the file, the OS also needs to change the bits of the blocks, right, a census file from 1 to 0.

60
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:41,000
So to reduce the overhead, the many OS maintains some additional disk structures to minimize this search operation.

61
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:53,000
The other way to keep track free space on the hard disk is to use the link list approach.

62
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:59,000
So free disk blocks are chained together by using a link list.

63
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:04,000
So this method has a negligible space overhead.

64
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:09,000
So unlike the bitmap, it doesn't require additional data structure.

65
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:21,000
So what OS need to maintain just a puncture, puncture to the starting block, the first block that's currently available on the hard disk.

66
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:29,000
Then that block contains the puncture, puncture to the next available block on the hard disk, and so on and so forth.

67
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:36,000
So if a location is a block at the time, right, for example, if you're using the link or index location,

68
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:52,000
then it can be done by simply choosing the free block at the height of the chain and adjust the first puncture, right, or the length value accordingly.

69
00:07:52,000 --> 00:08:05,000
However, if a location is contiguous, right, for instance, if you're using a first feed policy to locate the large enough hole on the hard disk to assign to the file,

70
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:18,000
right, then in this case, right, so we have to search the entire chain in order to locate the required number of contiguous free blocks.

71
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:27,000
So again, the puncture and the length value need to be adjusted and the link list of a free block need to be modified.

72
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:37,000
So this approach saves the space, but also has its own problem, right.

73
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:51,000
So every time OS allocate a block, then OS need to read the block first to recover the puncture to the next free block before writing data to that block.

74
00:08:51,000 --> 00:09:03,000
If many individual blocks need to be allocated at one time for a file operation, then this greatly slows down the operation.

75
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:13,000
So similarly, deleting a file whose blocks are at different places at the hard disk is also very, very time consuming,

76
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:23,000
because free up the block need to be added into the linked list in the order.

